    <li><a href="#global-overview">Global Homicide Rate Overview (1990–2023)</a></li>
    <li><a href="#regional-comparison">Regional &amp; Country-Level Comparison</a></li>
    <li><a href="#sex-disparity">Gender Disparity in Homicide Victimization</a></li>
    <li><a href="#age-group-analysis">Age Group Analysis</a></li>
    <li><a href="#notable-trends">Notable Country-Level Trends &amp; Outliers</a></li>
    <li><a href="#data-limitations">Data Limitations &amp; Policy Recommendations</a></li>
  </ol>
</div>

<!-- ============================================================ KPI DASHBOARD ============================================================ -->
<section id="kpis" class="section" style="background: linear-gradient(135deg, #1a3a5c 0%, #2c5f8a 100%); color: #fff; padding: 32px 40px;">
  <div class="section-num" style="color: rgba(255,255,255,0.6);">EXECUTIVE DASHBOARD</div>
  <h2 style="color: #fff; margin-bottom: 4px;">Key Performance Indicators</h2>
  <p style="color: rgba(255,255,255,0.65); font-size: 13px; margin-bottom: 24px;">Most recent available data (2022–2023) unless otherwise noted. Rates per 100,000 population.</p>
  <div class="kpi-grid">
    <div class="kpi-card" style="--kpi-color: #e74c3c;">
      <div class="kpi-label">Global Median Rate</div>
      <div class="kpi-value" style="color: #e74c3c;">2.11</div>
      <div class="kpi-desc">Per 100,000 population (2022–2023 reporters, n=108)</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card" style="--kpi-color: #3498db;">
      <div class="kpi-label">Male-to-Female Ratio</div>
      <div class="kpi-value" style="color: #3498db;">2.7×</div>
      <div class="kpi-desc">Males are 2.7× more likely to be homicide victims than females (median basis)</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card" style="--kpi-color: #c0392b;">
      <div class="kpi-label">Highest National Rate</div>
      <div class="kpi-value" style="color: #c0392b;">76.3</div>
      <div class="kpi-desc">Turks &amp; Caicos Islands, 2022 — 36× the global median</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card" style="--kpi-color: #27ae60;">
      <div class="kpi-label">Decade Change (2000s→2010s)</div>
      <div class="kpi-value" style="color: #27ae60;">−9.6%</div>
      <div class="kpi-desc">Global mean fell from 8.51 to 7.70 per 100k — the only clear decade-on-decade improvement</div>
    </div>
    <div class="kpi-card" style="--kpi-color: #e67e22;">
      <div class="kpi-label">Reporting Coverage (2023)</div>
      <div class="kpi-value" style="color: #e67e22;">41.8%</div>
      <div class="kpi-desc">Only 84 of 201 countries had 2023 data; coverage peaked at 80.6% in 2008</div>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div style="background: rgba(255,255,255,0.08); border-radius: 8px; padding: 14px 18px; font-size: 13px; color: rgba(255,255,255,0.75); margin-top: 8px;">
    <strong style="color:#fff;">⚠ Interpretation Note:</strong> The global median (2.11) is the preferred central measure — the mean (9.08) is heavily skewed by Caribbean and Latin American outliers. All figures derived directly from UNODC/data.csv; no values imputed or fabricated.
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============================================================ SECTION 1: GLOBAL OVERVIEW ============================================================ -->
<section id="global-overview" class="section">
  <div class="section-num">SECTION 1</div>
  <h2>Global Homicide Rate Overview</h2>
  <p class="section-intro">This section traces the trajectory of intentional homicide from 1990 to 2023 using the median and mean rate across all reporting countries each year, along with the number of reporting nations — a critical caveat for interpreting any trend.</p>

  <p>The global picture is one of modest but meaningful progress, punctuated by significant regional divergences. The <strong>median homicide rate across reporting countries declined from approximately 3.4 per 100,000 in 1990 to 1.8 in 2023</strong>, representing a roughly 46% reduction. However, this headline figure must be read alongside declining reporting coverage — by 2023, only 84 of 201 tracked nations submitted data, compared to a peak of 162 in 2008.</p>

  <div class="chart-wrap">
    <div class="chart-title">Global Intentional Homicide Rate Over Time (1990–2023) — Median &amp; Mean, All Reporting Countries</div>
    <canvas id="globalTrendChart" height="90"></canvas>
    <div class="chart-note">Shaded area represents the difference between mean (skewed by high-rate outliers) and median (robust central estimate). Rates per 100,000 population. Number of reporting countries shown as bar chart (right axis).</div>
  </div>

  <p>The <strong>mean rate</strong> tells a different story from the median. While the median has declined consistently, the mean has oscillated between 6.3 and 9.7 throughout the period, reflecting the outsized influence of a small number of extremely high-rate countries — particularly in the Caribbean and Central America. Countries like El Salvador (peak: 134 per 100k in 1994) can dramatically inflate the global mean without reflecting the experience of most nations.</p>

  <div class="callout success">
    <strong>Key Finding:</strong> The world has seen genuine progress in reducing typical national homicide rates — the median fell by ~46% between 1990 and 2023. However, <strong>high-violence nations have not converged toward the global median</strong>, meaning the burden remains sharply concentrated in a minority of countries, particularly in the Caribbean and Latin America.
  </div>

  <h3>Decade-over-Decade Analysis</h3>
  <div class="two-col">
    <div>
      <p>Breaking the data into decades reveals that the <strong>2010s were the only decade with a clear, sustained decline</strong> in the global mean homicide rate (−9.6% vs. the 2000s). The 1990s and 2000s saw relatively stable — and even slightly rising — global averages, reflecting the post-Soviet violence spike in Eastern Europe and concurrent escalation in parts of the Americas.</p>
      <p>The 2020s data (through 2023) show near-flat trends at around 7.7 per 100,000 mean, with narrowing coverage complicating interpretation. The COVID-19 pandemic (2020–2021) introduced disruptions to reporting systems and potentially to crime patterns themselves.</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <div class="chart-wrap" style="margin: 0;">
        <div class="chart-title">Mean Homicide Rate by Decade</div>
        <canvas id="decadeChart" height="180"></canvas>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="table-wrap" style="margin-top: 20px;">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Decade</th><th>Global Mean Rate</th><th>Change vs. Prior Decade</th><th>Context</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>1990s</td><td>8.27</td><td>—</td><td>Post-Soviet violence spike; Latin American peaks</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2000s</td><td>8.51</td><td><span class="trend-up">+2.9%</span></td><td>Expanding reporting; Central American gang violence</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2010s</td><td>7.70</td><td><span class="trend-down">−9.6%</span></td><td>Declining in Eastern Europe, parts of Asia; mixed Americas</td></tr>
        <tr><td>2020s*</td><td>7.71</td><td><span class="trend-up">+0.2%</span></td><td>*Partial decade (2020–2023); COVID disruption; coverage declining</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
</section>

<!-- ============================================================ SECTION 2: REGIONAL ============================================================ -->
<section id="regional-comparison" class="section">
  <div class="section-num">SECTION 2</div>
  <h2>Regional &amp; Country-Level Comparison</h2>
  <p class="section-intro">Homicide risk is profoundly unequal across the globe. This section identifies the regions and countries at the extremes, drawing on the latest available year per country (2022–2023 data where available).</p>

  <h3>Regional Overview</h3>
  <div class="two-col-3-2">
    <div>
      <p>The regional breakdown (using the most recent available data per country) reveals a stark global divide. <strong>The Caribbean and Latin America stand apart from every other world region</strong>, with median rates of 27.1 and 11.2 per 100,000 respectively — versus just 1.1 for Europe and 0.5 for East Asia.</p>
      <p>Africa, while often assumed to be among the world's most violent regions, has a median rate of 4.7 — below North America's 5.8 and substantially below the Caribbean. This reflects the heterogeneous nature of African reporting (dominated by relatively stable southern and eastern African nations), and the influence of high-rate outliers such as South Africa (43.7) and Lesotho.</p>
      <p>East Asia and the Middle East represent the safest regions by median rates, driven by countries with strong state capacity, low firearm availability, and robust criminal justice systems (notably Japan, Singapore, South Korea, and Qatar).</p>
    </div>
    <div>
      <div class="chart-wrap" style="margin: 0;">
        <div class="chart-title">Median Homicide Rate by Region (Latest Year per Country)</div>
        <canvas id="regionalBarChart" height="260"></canvas>
        <div class="chart-note">Rates per 100,000. Based on latest available year per country. Region groupings use ISO codes.</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <h3>Top 10 Highest-Rate Countries (2022–2023)</h3>
  <div class="callout warn">
    <strong>Note on Small Territories:</strong> Several small island territories (Turks &amp; Caicos, Saint Kitts and Nevis) appear with very high rates, in part because small population sizes amplify per-capita statistics from a small absolute number of homicides. These figures are valid but should be interpreted with caution when comparing to larger nations.
  </div>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th class="rank">#</th><th>Country / Territory</th><th>Year</th><th>Rate (per 100,000)</th><th>Region</th></tr></thead>
