  <p>The male predominance in homicide victimization reflects multiple intersecting factors: male overrepresentation in criminal networks and gang activity, higher participation in high-risk occupations and environments, and cultural norms around masculinity and conflict resolution. The gap is most extreme in countries with high gang and organized crime activity.</p>

  <p>Notably, <strong>female homicide rates are not negligible</strong>. Countries like South Africa (12.2 per 100,000 for women) and Haiti (8.7) have female rates that exceed the overall homicide rate of many nations. In South Africa, the female rate alone ranks higher than the total rate for approximately 70% of the world's countries.</p>

